Title: What They Called Recovery
Credit: Animated feature screenplay — scene draft
Author: Christopher Kokkinos
Draft date: 2026-09-28
Status: SC012 proposed for individual author scene review; combined pass provisionally accepted

INT. ELIAS'S CAR - MOTORWAY - PRESENT DAY

Zoe keeps her backpack on her lap. The road passes beneath them.

ZOE
It was an ordinary Tuesday. I hadn't arranged to meet anyone. I was walking home.

Elias listens. The sound of traffic carries into her memory.

INT./EXT. HELEN AND BRIAN'S HOUSE/SCHOOL - TUESDAY - ZOE'S ACCOUNT

HELEN calls up the stairs. ZOE, thirteen, appears with one shoe on. BRIAN has already left. Over burned toast Helen teases her about the missing shoe. At school JACKIE returns Zoe's science book, then joins her on the wall behind the sports hall. Their shared joke about a teacher lasts until the final bell. Jackie crosses the road first.

JACKIE
Message me when you get home.

ZOE
You message me.

Jackie smiles. This is a familiar argument with no stakes yet.

INT./EXT. HELEN'S KITCHEN/CORNER SHOP/STREET - LATE AFTERNOON - ZOE'S ACCOUNT

Helen discovers there is no milk and gives Zoe money. Zoe negotiates an extra pound for chocolate. Four familiar streets separate the shop and home. She pays for a milk bottle and chocolate, then walks past the boarded florist, post box and stone dog.

A white unmarked van slows beside her. The side door opens. Two adults pull Zoe inside. The milk strikes the pavement and spreads along the kerb. The door slams before she can see home.

INT. VAN/GARAGE/HOUSE - TUESDAY - ZOE'S ACCOUNT

Held inside the moving van, Zoe stops kicking to breathe. She counts a left turn, a long road, a roundabout, two close speed bumps and a right, restarting whenever fear breaks her count. The van enters a garage; a shutter rattles closed before she is led out.

Through a front-door window: a trimmed lawn and a child's bicycle. Inside: drawn curtains, a locked cabinet of numbered folders. Zoe glimpses a safe through a partly open study door before a man shuts it.

A WOMAN sets down a glass of water Zoe did not request.

WOMAN
You'll be processed tomorrow.

ZOE
For what?

No answer. Upstairs Zoe checks an empty wardrobe, a high window that barely opens, and a lock on the outside of her door. Alone on the bed, she hears footsteps and returns her gaze to the milk image in her memory.

INT. ELIAS'S CAR - MOTORWAY - PRESENT DAY

ELIAS
Nobody had arranged to meet you? What could you see on the journey?

ZOE
I was buying milk. I counted turns. Not enough to find the place from the route. It was still light when we arrived.

ELIAS
Did you know Cain's name then?

ZOE
Not yet.

He lets the silence stand, then checks the mirror.

ELIAS
Keep going when you're ready. I'm listening.

Zoe decides to continue.
